Setting up the environment - create linux style env, create and set up python project, add jupyter support and run the notebook
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
we will use uv tools to set up the environment:
uv is a modern package manager created by the same team behind Ruff (a popular Python linter). It is designed to be:
Extremely fast (written in Rust, not Python)
Smarter at resolving dependency conflicts (like the terraform-compliance error you saw earlier)
Easier for managing projects long-term

1. install WSL (Windows Subsystem for Linux ) first

wsl --install

This will:
Install WSL 2
Install Ubuntu (a Linux distribution)
Ask you to restart your computer - After restart, Ubuntu will open and ask you to create a username and password (this is different from your Windows account).

                
2. Open your WSL terminal (Ubuntu)
Search for "Ubuntu" in your Windows Start menu
Click to open it — you will see a purple/black terminal
                
if you dont see ubuntu (sometimes ubunto is not installed) - Open Command Prompt or PowerShell and run 
wsl --list --online     (to see what distributions are available)
This will show you a list of all Linux distributions you can install

You should see ubuntu in the list 
Run this to install ubuntu
wsl --install -d Ubuntu
When it opens for the first time, it will ask you to create a Linux username and password

3. Install uv from ubuntu terminal
curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh

4. Create a project directory(dev/projects) and initialise the project - claude-demo
cd ~
mkdir -p dev/projects
cd dev/projects
uv init claude-demo
cd claude-demo

5. Install Jupyter support
uv add --dev ipykernel

6. create the isolated kernel ((Replace yourusername with the username you created in Step 1)
uv run python -m ipykernel install --user --name claude-python3 --display-name "Claude Python 3"


7. Create your notebook folder inside the project and start Jupyter
mkdir jupyter
cd jupyter
uv run --with jupyter jupyter lab

you will see something like
http://localhost:8888/lab?token=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
Hold Ctrl + Click on that link, and Jupyter Lab will open in your browser! 

Stop the notebook server: do ctrl + C in ubuntu terninal to stop the jupyter engine
start the notebook: goto ubunto, navigate to project > jupyter, start the engine - uv run --with jupyter jupyter lab     

8. Run jupyter in vs code
Stop Jupyter by pressing Ctrl + C and typing y
Type this command: code .   (his tells Windows: "Open VS Code right here in this folder inside Linux.)

Once VS Code opens, it might ask you to install the "WSL" extension or "Python" extension. Click Install on any pop-ups it shows you. 
Specifically, make sure you have these in the Extensions view (the icon looks like 4 squares on the left):
Python
Jupyter
On the left side of VS Code, you will see your file list. Click on your renamed notebook. It will open up looking very similar to the browser version.

Select the "Brain" (Kernel)
In the top-right corner of VS Code, you will see a button that says Select Kernel. Click it.
Select Python Environments...
Look for the one that mentions your project folder and says (.venv) next to it.
Select that one.
