The below work would be done inside the notebook

Create a .env File
In Jupyter Lab in your browser, look at the left sidebar. You should see a folder icon at the top. Make sure you can see your file list.
Create a New File
Right-click in the empty space of the file list on the left
Select New File
Name it .env (including the dot at the start — this is important!)
Press Enter

Note: In Linux (which is what your Ubuntu app is running), any file that starts with a dot (like .env) is automatically considered a "hidden" file. 
Jupyter Lab's file browser gets confused when you try to name a visible file into a hidden one. In that case
create this file directly from inside your notebook.

Open your notebook. Click on a new, empty cell.
Type 
%%writefile .env
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=your-api-key-here  (Replace your-api-key-here with "your-api-key-here")
and run

How to actually see the .env file in the left sidebar
Because it's a "hidden" file, Jupyter Lab hides it by default so your workspace looks clean. If you ever want to see it or edit it manually:
Look at the very top menu bar in Jupyter Lab.
Click on View.
Check the box that says Show Hidden Files.
Now you will see your .env file sitting right there in your file list
or
In the file browser on the left sidebar:
Right-click in the empty space of the file list
See if there's an option called "Show Hidden Files" in the context menu
or
Inside your ubuntu terminal, navigate to your folder and run 
ls -la (to list all the files)

